<item>
  <title>A Deep Learning Approach to Antibiotic Discovery</title>
  <dc:creator>Ben </dc:creator>
  <link>https://benjaminhan.net/posts/20200223-antibiotic-halicin/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=feed</link>
  <description><![CDATA[<p><a href="https://benjaminhan.net/posts/20200223-antibiotic-halicin/?utm_source=rss&amp;utm_medium=feed">Read on benjaminhan.net →</a></p>
 





<div class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="https://benjaminhan.net/posts/20200223-antibiotic-halicin/cover.jpg" class="img-fluid figure-img"></p>
<figcaption>A Deep Learning Approach to Antibiotic Discovery. Image from <a href="https://news.mit.edu/2020/artificial-intelligence-identifies-new-antibiotic-0220">MIT News</a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<p>Halcin: the first antibiotic discovered with AI/DNN.</p>
<blockquote class="blockquote">
<p>First, we trained a deep neural network model to predict growth inhibition of Escherichia coli using a collection of 2,335 molecules. Second, we applied the resulting model to several discrete chemical libraries, comprising &gt;107 million molecules, to identify potential lead compounds with activity against E. coli. After ranking the compounds according to the model’s predicted score, we lastly selected a list of candidates based on a pre-specified prediction score threshold, chemical structure, and availability.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr>
<p><em>Originally posted on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/benjaminhan_a-deep-learning-approach-to-antibiotic-discovery-activity-6637189337257771008-1VDX">LinkedIn</a>.</em></p>



 ]]></description>
